  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard or waited for the rule to run. Its status is still \"pending\".\n\n**Data:** The data view `Kibana Sample Data Logs` covers `kibana_sample_data_logs` with `timestamp` as its time field. Its main fields are `response`, `extension`, `bytes`, `clientip`, `geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `machine.os`, `host`, `url` and `request`. `response` is a string field, so I used its `.keyword` subfield to match status codes.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-c4257d7d Web logs overview\" has four panels:\n- Total requests\n- Requests over time\n- Requests by response code\n- Average bytes by extension\n\nYou can open it at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-c4257d7d-web-logs-overview-2c197740cb2e.\n\n**Rule:** \"EFF-c4257d7d 5xx spike\" is an Elasticsearch query rule that runs every minute. It counts documents where `response.keyword` matches `5*` over the last 5 minutes. It fires when that count is above 10. I didn't attach any actions, so a firing rule only creates an alert in Kibana and doesn't notify anyone.",
